\section{Decode before reasoning about types}

Verification establishes which abstract machine states can reach each instruction. It is not a substitute for bounded parsing. First decode the method, record the start offset of every instruction, and check operand lengths, local indices, and constant-pool entry kinds. Branch destinations must be instruction starts, not merely offsets in range; for an exception region the exclusive end may also be the end of the code. Check these structural conditions even in bytes a normal execution would not reach. Only then build a control-flow graph including exception handlers. For a call that throws, for example, the handler does not inherit the caller's partially consumed operand stack.

The \emph{Java Virtual Machine Specification} by Lindholm et al.\ distinguishes type checking with stack map frames from the older inference-based verification regime. Phillip M.\ Yelland's \emph{A Compositional Account of the Java Virtual Machine}, a historical model, builds instructions from smaller typed operations. This is a useful implementation discipline: sharing a typed pop rule between arithmetic instructions prevents one handler from silently treating an integer as a reference. The rule definitions, not the written instructions in the input file, determine what type transfer is legal.

\section{Type states and evidence at joins}

For a simplified verifier use $(L,S)$, a vector of local verification types and an ordered operand stack. A simulation of \texttt{iadd} requires two integers, pops them, and pushes one integer. At a join, incoming operand heights must agree; reference types may widen to a common assignable type, but an integer cannot turn into a reference. Where inference is used, put a block back on the worklist whenever its abstract entry state widens. A finite type domain of bounded frame size and monotone rules gives termination for this model. Reject reads of a local whose merged state does not establish the required type.

With stack maps, check a prepared target state rather than computing all joins from scratch. Start with an entry frame derived from the method signature and check that every reachable edge produces a state assignable to the advertised target. A frame is a claim, not an instruction to coerce the incoming values. In the JVM the \texttt{StackMapTable} compactly encodes frame changes and distances. An allocation before constructor completion is represented by an uninitialized verification type tied to its allocation site (with a special rule for \texttt{uninitializedThis}); normal completion of the constructor call changes aliases of that value in the abstract frame to its initialized type. It is not enough for the aliases to have the same eventual class.

\section{Worked check: two ways to reach an integer return}

In the following static method the only parameter, local 0, is an integer. The branch consumes the condition. Labels represent instruction starts, not byte offsets:

\begin{verbatim}
    iload_0
    ifeq zero
    iconst_2
    goto done
zero:
    iconst_1
done:
    ireturn
\end{verbatim}

At \texttt{zero} the claimed stack is empty and local 0 is still an integer. At \texttt{done} it has one integer, whether the predecessor branches from \texttt{goto} or falls through. Changing \texttt{iconst\_1} to \texttt{aconst\_null} must cause rejection at the edge to \texttt{done}, even if the supplied frame still advertises one integer. Removing that push entirely checks height agreement. A separate handler fixture should give the handler a single caught exception on entry regardless of temporary values on the normal operand stack. Keep positive and negative tests for all three checks; accepting only perfectly compiler-produced input will not exercise the verifier's obligations.

\section{Where the proof stops}

Hanbing Liu and J.\ Strother Moore's \emph{Executable JVM model for analytical reasoning: A study} presents M6, a JVM model informed by the constrained KVM implementation. An executable model is useful as an independent oracle on shared instruction subsets: compare transitions from abstract frames with observed values of well-typed executions. Do not confuse a verification stack map, which describes \emph{types in bytecode frames}, with a collector map, which describes \emph{live physical locations} at a particular machine-code safepoint (Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-245}). Nor does verification prove termination, authorize I/O, or remove the need for dynamic array bounds checks. The Modern Compiler Dossier's Chapter~29, \emph{Proof-Carrying IR and Certified Rewrite Contracts}, asks a related question at a different boundary: what evidence must a producer supply, and exactly what does the consumer's checker establish?
